# This file REPLACES semgrep's built-in ignore list; it does not extend it.
# That is the opposite of what this comment used to say, and the difference
# matters: the built-in list drops `tests/`. Measured on 2026-08-28 with
# `semgrep scan --config p/python .` -- with this file present, 77 of 77
# tracked Python files are scanned and 1 is skipped; with it moved aside, 46
# are scanned and 32 are skipped, all 31 files in `tests/` among them.
#
# So the one exclusion below is the only exclusion in force, and this
# repository's tests are in scope. They are gates, and a fixture is where a
# secret or an injection pattern most often hides. Keep it that way: adding a
# broad path here silently takes back the coverage this file is what provides.
#
# datasets/riverbend-demo/interface.html is a static HTML fixture -- a
# captured structural snapshot for the accessibility suite to check, not a
# served page and not a Django template. Its `<form method="post">` false-
# positives python.django.security.django-no-csrf-token, which fires on the
# HTML pattern regardless of whether Django is anywhere in the stack. The
# file cannot carry an inline nosemgrep comment instead: it is content-hashed
# into datasets/riverbend-demo/checksums.json, and editing it would move that
# hash the same way editing src/plumbline/ moves harness_source_sha256 (see
# pyproject.toml's own comment on F541 for that pattern) -- for one demo
# fixture, not the whole suite, but the same "don't touch checksummed
# evidence for a linter" reasoning applies.
datasets/riverbend-demo/interface.html
